# Go leaves a binary next to its source when built inside a cmd
# directory, named after the directory.  None of them belong in git.
/cmd/*/slgo
/cmd/*/slgo-*
/cmd/*/slgod
/cmd/*/slsh
/cmd/*/slrun
/cmd/*/slbench
/cmd/*/slbotd
/cmd/*/slhash
/cmd/*/sl-host
/cmd/*/msggen
/cmd/*/md
/msggen

# And in the root, which is where "go build ./cmd/slsh" leaves it.
#
# A new command wants both: its name here and under /cmd/*/ above.  Two
# compiled binaries were committed once and lived in the history for
# months -- 36 MB of them -- because neither list had heard of them.
/slsh
/slgod
/slrun
/slbench
/slbotd
/slhash
/sl-host
/msggen
/slgo
/slgo-multiattach
/slchat
# The binary from "go build ./cmd/md", not the md/ package.  A trailing
# slash on the exception is what keeps the directory in the tree;
# without it the command's name would hide the renderer.
/md
!/md/

# slrun and slbench were automate and autobench until 2026-08-22.  A
# checkout from before the rename has their binaries in it, and taking
# the old names off this list un-ignored them: the check refused the
# rename itself because of two sitting here.  They stay.
/automate
/autobench
/cmd/*/automate
/cmd/*/autobench

# Linden Lab's message template.  msggen fetches it; a copy pulled down
# for offline work is theirs and does not belong in this repository.
/message_template.msg

# Anything else built here.
*.o
*.a
*.test
*.out

# Review notes written against this tree.  They quote what they found,
# which means they quote the live grid -- real avatars, real parcels --
# and check-identities is the only thing standing between one of them
# and a public repository.  It caught this file being swept up by
# "git add -A", twice.  Working notes about the code are not the code.
/*-audit-*.md
/*-review-*.md

# Notes written while raising coverage: what is still uncovered and why.
# They are working notes about the tests, not documentation of the code.
/coverage-notes/

# The issue log: what is known to be wrong with this tree and has not
# been fixed yet, one file per fault and an index over them.  It quotes
# the live grid for the same reason the review notes above do -- an
# issue is only worth anything if it says what was actually seen -- so
# it stays out of the repository.  See issues/README.md.
/issues/

# handbook/bundle.py writes the one-file build here by default; it is
# published, not kept.
/handbook/handbook-bundle.html
